.TH MICROIDE 1 "2026-06-20" "microide" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
microide \- compact native desktop IDE shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B microide
[\fB--disable-plugins\fR] [\fB--safe-mode\fR] [\fB--control\fR] [\fB--set\fR \fIid value\fR]... [\fB--control-spec\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB--version\fR] [\fIproject-path\fR]
.br
.B microide control-send
[\fB--pid\fR \fIn\fR | \fB--socket\fR \fIpath\fR] (\fIcommand\fR... | \fB--query\fR \fIverb\fR | \fB--json\fR \fIobject\fR) [\fB--wait\fR \fIevent\fR] [\fB--timeout\fR \fIsecs\fR]
.br
.B microide control-help
.br
.B microide control-commands
.br
.B microide control-list
.br
.B microide dump-state
.I persisted-file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B microide
is a single-window desktop editor and IDE shell (editor, compare, merge,
search, git, terminal, plugin, and debugger workflows). With no
.I project-path
it opens the current working directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --disable-plugins
Start without loading the Lua plugin runtime.
.TP
.B --safe-mode
Start without plugins and without restoring the previous session.
.TP
.B --control
Force-start the control channel and mirror every response/event to stdout as
JSONL (the headless entry point), independent of the
.B control.enabled
setting.
.TP
.B --set \fIid value\fR
Apply a transient (never-persisted) setting override. May be repeated.
.TP
.B --control-spec \fIfile\fR
Apply a cold-start control spec at launch: open a project with breakpoints
already set (and optionally reveal files / start a debug session) before the
window becomes interactive. See
.B microide control-help
for the JSON schema. Spec line numbers are 1-based; relative paths resolve
against the project root.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print the microide version and exit.
.SH CONTROL CHANNEL
An external tool (such as an LLM) can drive a running instance over a private
Unix-domain socket. The one-shot client
.B microide control-send
is the easiest way in. The full protocol, query verbs, commands, control-send
usage, and end-to-end recipes follow verbatim from
.BR "microide control-help" :
.PP
.nf
microide control channel
========================

An external tool (e.g. an LLM) can drive a running microide instance: set
breakpoints, step through a debug session, open/close files, switch projects,
open terminals, and observe where execution stopped. The one-shot CLI
`microide control-send` is the easiest way in -- no socket plumbing required.

Debug a program in 4 steps (copy-paste)
---------------------------------------
  microide /path/to/project --set control.enabled true &   # window + live socket
  microide control-send --query adapters                   # what can debug (gdb is bundled)
  microide control-send breakpoint-function-add main       # debugger auto-enables
  microide control-send debug-run ./build/app --wait stopped  # launch; block until it stops
control-send auto-discovers the sole running instance, sends one request, prints
every JSONL reply/event to stdout, and exits (0 reply-ok, 1 reply-not-ok, 2
usage/connect, 3 --wait timed out). `debug-run <program> [args...]` needs no
pre-defined launch config; `--wait stopped` returns once execution halts. Leave
the window open to hand control to a human.

Enabling
--------
The channel is OFF by default. Three ways to turn it on:
  microide --control                 force-start + mirror JSONL to stdout (headless)
  microide --set control.enabled true start the socket (transient, not persisted)
  Settings overlay (Ctrl+,)          flip `control.enabled` (persisted)
It starts immediately on the running instance -- no restart needed.

Headless agent stream (microide --control [--control-spec <file>])
-----------------------------------------------------------------
--control force-starts the channel AND mirrors every response/event to stdout as
JSONL, so an agent can drive the instance with no socket client. The stream order:
  {"event":"ready","pid":..,"socket":"..","project_root":".."}  (handshake, first)
  {"applied":"<command>","ok":true|false,"error":".."}        (one per spec entry)
  {"event":"output"|"stopped"|"terminated",...}              (as the session runs)
--set <id> <value> applies a transient (never-persisted) setting override and may
be repeated; an explicit project (positional path or spec `project`) wins over a
restored session.

Discovery
---------
Each running instance with the channel on listens on a private Unix domain
socket (mode 0600) under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/microide/ and writes a descriptor
file to $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/microide/instances/<pid>.json:
  {"pid":..,"socket":"..","project_root":"..","project_hash":".."}
Run `microide control-list` to print descriptors for all running instances.

One-shot client (microide control-send)
---------------------------------------
Sends a single request to a running instance and prints the JSONL reply (and,
with --wait, any events) to stdout. Auto-targets the sole running instance; use
--pid or --socket to disambiguate. This is the reliable way to drive the channel
from a script -- it keeps the connection open until the reply arrives.
  microide control-send <command...>          # {"command":"<joined args>"}
  microide control-send --query <verb> [--args <json>]
  microide control-send --json '<object>'     # sent verbatim
  options: --pid <n> | --socket <path> | --timeout <secs> | --wait <event> | --id <n>
Exit codes: 0 reply ok, 1 reply not ok, 2 usage/discovery/connect, 3 --wait timeout.

Protocol (newline-delimited JSON; one object per line)
------------------------------------------------------
Request (run a command):
  {"id":1,"command":"breakpoint-set src/main.cpp 42"}
Request (read state):
  {"id":2,"query":"debug-state"}
Response (command):
  {"id":1,"ok":true,"feedback":"..."}   or   {"id":1,"ok":false,"error":"..."}
Response (query):
  {"id":2,"ok":true,"result":{...}}
Unsolicited events (pushed to every connected client):
  {"event":"stopped","file":"main.cpp","line":42,"reason":"breakpoint","frames":[...]}
  {"event":"terminated","sessionId":..}
  {"event":"output","category":"stdout","text":".."}
Lines are 1-based for the developer-facing surface (commands, spec, events);
they map to microide's internal 0-based buffer lines automatically.

Query verbs
-----------
  debug-state
  breakpoints
  function-breakpoints
  exception-filters
  tabs
  projects
  status
  launch-configs
  adapters

Commands
--------
Any command from the command palette works. Run `microide control-commands` for
the full list. Breakpoint commands added for headless control:
  breakpoint-set <file> <line> [condition]
  breakpoint-remove <file> <line>
  breakpoint-enable <file> <line>
  breakpoint-disable <file> <line>
  breakpoint-condition <file> <line> [expr]      (omit expr to clear)
  breakpoint-hit-condition <file> <line> [expr]  (omit expr to clear)
  breakpoint-logmessage <file> <line> [message]  (omit message to clear)
  breakpoint-clear [file]                         (omit file to clear all)
Function breakpoints (break by symbol name -- no file/line needed):
  breakpoint-function-add <name>
  breakpoint-function-remove <name>
  breakpoint-function-toggle <name>
  breakpoint-function-condition <name> [expr]    (omit expr to clear)
  breakpoint-exception-condition <filterId> [expr]
Launch a session:
  debug-run [--type <adapter>] <program> [args...]  ad-hoc launch by program path
                                                    (no pre-defined config needed)
  debug-launch [name]                               start a named launch config
Debug control: debug-start, debug-continue, debug-step-over, debug-step-in,
  debug-step-out, debug-pause, debug-restart, debug-stop. Reverse execution
  (recording adapters only): debug-reverse-continue, debug-step-back.
  (Over the control channel, any breakpoint-/debug- command auto-enables the
  debugger transiently -- no separate debug.enabled prelude is needed.)

Review / diff workflows (open tabs to review changes)
----------------------------------------------------
These verbs switch to the Source Control view and bulk-open diff/merge tabs for
review. They dedupe against already-open tabs and close stale (clean) review tabs
from a prior run; dirty (edited) review/merge tabs are always kept. The reply
feedback lists the files that were opened.
  review-conflicts     one merge tab per conflicted file in the working tree.
                       NON-MUTATING -- run the merge yourself first, then call it:
                         microide control-send term "git merge origin/main"
                         microide control-send review-conflicts
  review-branch [ref]  one compare tab (working tree vs <ref>) per differing file
                       -- "what is different between local state and <ref>". <ref>
                       is any commit-ish (branch, tag, hash, HEAD~3). Omit it (or
                       pass `origin`) to use the repo base branch (origin/HEAD,
                       else main/master, else @{upstream}).
  review-commit [commit]  one compare tab (<commit>~1 vs <commit>) per file that
                       commit changed; <commit> is any commit-ish and defaults to
                       HEAD (review the last commit). Pass a hash to review any
                       historical commit.

Recipes (end-to-end agent workflows)
------------------------------------
1) Open a session, break in a function, hand the live window to the human:
   microide /path/to/project --set control.enabled true &   # normal window + socket
   microide control-send breakpoint-function-add A          # debugger auto-enables
   microide control-send debug-run ./build/app              # or: debug-launch <config>
   # leave the window open -- the human now drives it interactively.
   (--set control.enabled true opens a NORMAL interactive window with the socket
    live; that is the path for "give me control". --control, by contrast, is the
    headless stream below.)
2) Investigate a crash, break just before the suspect line (headless):
   microide /path/to/project --control --control-spec spec.json
   # spec: {"breakpoints":[{"file":"src/foo.c","line":N}],"launch":"<config>"}
   # the debugger auto-enables; read launch names first via the launch-configs
   # query. Watch stdout for {"event":"stopped",..} then debug-continue/step.
   # (functionBreakpoints in the spec break by symbol name with no file/line.)
3) Merge a branch and open every conflict for resolution:
   microide control-send term "git merge origin/main"   # you run the merge
   microide control-send review-conflicts                # opens a merge tab each
4) Review what an agent just coded (the last commit):
   microide control-send review-commit                   # HEAD~1 vs HEAD, one tab/file

Cold-start spec (microide --control-spec <file.json>)
-----------------------------------------------------
Open a project with breakpoints already set before the window is interactive:
  {
    "project": "/path/to/project",          // optional; selects the project
    "settings": [["control.enabled","true"]], // optional transient overrides
    "breakpoints": [
      {"file":"src/main.cpp","line":42},
      {"file":"src/util.cpp","line":120,"condition":"x>10"},
      {"file":"src/log.cpp","line":7,"logMessage":"hit {x}"},
      {"file":"src/a.cpp","line":3,"hitCondition":">=5","enabled":false}
    ],
    "functionBreakpoints": [                  // optional; break by symbol name
      {"name":"main"},
      {"name":"process","condition":"n>3","enabled":false}
    ],
    "open": ["src/main.cpp"],               // optional files to reveal
    "launch": "Python: main",                // optional launch-config to auto-start
    "commands": ["sidebar-hide"]            // optional raw command lines, run last
  }
Spec line numbers are 1-based; relative file paths resolve against the project
root. `settings` apply transiently and first; the debugger auto-enables when the
spec has breakpoints or a launch. `settings` also accepts an object form
({"id":"value"}). Discover names without reading plugin source via the
`launch-configs` and `adapters` queries.
.fi
